r seem to me to be distinctly  expressionist  but it's the subtler more muted use of  expressionism that begin to appear in  the film noirs that has spread far and  wide into film and tv to this day from  apocalypse now to train spotting there  are many films where scenes or portions  of the film seem to exist in a space  that isn't quite reality where the  architecture sets lighting and general  atmosphere are heightened and this  heightening or shifting of reality is a  reflection of the character's  psychological experiences  while last year the tragedy of macbeth  was explicitly nodding to expressionism  the humans uses it in a more subtle way  to portray the character's anxiety about  decay and aging  it's almost as if the apartment the film  is set in is sick or diseased  [Music]  since season one euphoria has been  making the term neon noir rattle around  in my head but i couldn't quite place a  finger exactly on why the show has none  of the plot elements or stereotypical  characters of war films but what are  these stunning shifts in lighting and  evocatively lit environments that often  appear in euphoria if not an external  manifestation of these characters  internal feelings euphoria feels like a  noir not because of its content but  because it harnesses expressionism like  the classic noirs did  while expressionism has been an  incredibly influential force in film i  feel like the echoes of expressionism  and film and tv don't get recognized as  such in part because it's been a century  since the movement and unless you've  gone to film school or are a hardcore  cinephile you probably haven't seen many  of the original german expressionist  films some of the directors working now  who are using these techniques may not  even be drawing direct influence from  the german expressionists but simply  from the many films since then that were  inspired by them but german  expressionism brought to screen an idea  about how you could approach lighting  and set d